</div>
<!--Q5-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Explain your reasoning for the equivalent equations in number 4.
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Your answer may vary, but here are some samples.
    </para>
    <list>
      <item>The equation \(3a + 6=15\) can be divided by 3 on both sides and becomes \(a +2 = 5\).</item>
      <item>When 6 is subtracted from both sides of the equation \(3a +6 = 15\), the equation becomes 3a = 9.</item>
      <item>When the equation \(3a + 6 = 15\) has 6 subtracted from both sides and becomes \(3a = 9\), then both sides are
        divided by 9, the equivalent equation is \(\frac{1}{3} a = 1\).</item>
    </list>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
